WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.563
The sheriff calls us outlaws.

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:04.700
But I say we are free.

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:07.270
Unlike some other Robin Hoods,

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:09.474
I can speak with an English accent.

00:00:09.474 --> 00:00:10.307
("God Save the Queen")

00:00:10.307 --> 00:00:12.620
[Narrator] There are countless
examples of American actors

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:14.646
who do terrible British accents.

00:00:14.646 --> 00:00:17.440
I've seen many strange things already.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:19.283
Mary Poppins, you look beautiful.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:21.760
[Narrator] While most
British actors appear

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:24.310
to have perfected their American accents.

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:27.133
Is that the going rate for
saving the woman you love?

00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:28.433
But, I'm gonna kill you.

00:00:29.810 --> 00:00:33.780
[Narrator] So why do Americans
have such a tough time?

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:35.883
I think part of it is an illusion.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:40.000
I think part of it is just
the fact that we actually hear

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.260
a lot more British actors
doing American accents.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.080
[Narrator] Erik Singer is a dialect coach

00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:46.080
based in New York.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:48.490
How many accents and dialects can I do?

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:49.380
All of them.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:50.520
(laughs)

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:52.790
[Narrator] He works with actors
to fine tune their accents

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:54.160
for film and TV.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:55.260
I think we can probably think

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:58.010
of some accents from British
actors in American accents

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:00.120
that are less successful.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.260
I can't imagine a world without you.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:04.190
There are guys you can hit.

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:05.680
And there's guys you can't.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:07.790
The British actors who
do really successful

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:11.630
American accents tend
to live in the states

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:14.340
tend to come here and
really be sort of be aiming

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.670
at the kind of success that, you know,

00:01:17.670 --> 00:01:20.510
having a career in TV and film

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:22.330
that's produced in Hollywood represent.

00:01:22.330 --> 00:01:24.740
The American actors who do
really good British accents,

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:25.790
and there are plenty,

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:29.730
tend to be under the
radar a little bit more.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:31.730
You know they're doing theater in London

00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:33.220
or something like that.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:34.990
[Narrator] The type of
training actors receive

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:36.410
can also come into play.

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:40.480
More British actors tend to
have a drama school training

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:43.430
in which speech and accents
and phonetics is part of it.

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:45.300
[Narrator] So what are
some examples of Americans

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.380
who can do a good British accent?

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:50.020
I've heard some clips of Michael C. Hall

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:52.700
in the Netflix series "Safe"
doing an English accent

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:54.708
that sounds really really really good.

00:01:54.708 --> 00:01:56.670
I'm looking for Jenny, my daughter,

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:59.420
I think she was Chris
last night, is she here?

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:04.110
Alan Tudyk does a great job,
he's the robot in "Rogue One."

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:06.890
I'm a reprogrammed Imperial Droid.

00:02:06.890 --> 00:02:08.560
[Narrator] On the flip side, Singer says

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:11.300
there are numerous British
actors who have really nailed

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:12.740
their American accents.

00:02:12.740 --> 00:02:17.520
Idris Elba's kind of African
American accent in "The Wire."

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.143
You going out on point pickin'
up business in the pit.

00:02:20.143 --> 00:02:21.620
Ain't nothin' else to it.

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:25.476
Daniel Kaluuya did an amazing
job, I think, in "Get Out."

00:02:25.476 --> 00:02:28.390
Yo, my man, they were asking me about

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:29.550
the African American experience.

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:30.810
Maybe you can take this one.

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:33.500
Mark Rylance, his native
accent is English.

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:36.100
He usually does a really
great job in American accents.

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:38.290
Hello, I am James Halliday,

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:40.430
if you're watching this, I'm dead.

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:41.510
[Narrator] For those who have struggled

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:42.810
with accents in the past.

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:45.120
I need that money, Tom.

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:46.400
[Narrator] Singer has some tips

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:48.670
for actors looking to hone their skills.

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:51.150
Listen to a native speaker, listen a lot

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:52.160
and listen some more.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:54.840
It's an act of the imagination
taking on another accent

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.720
if you're not familiar with
the culture, the place,

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:00.060
the people or you don't
have a strong kind of sense

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:02.400
of empathetic identification

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.040
that's when it gets really hard.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:06.610
Meryl Streep is famous for
having done this a lot,

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:11.420
being surrounded by people
who talk in the target accent.

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:16.170
1941, when Japan attacked Pearl Harbor.

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:17.610
Meryl Streep doing Margaret Thatcher

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:20.280
which is a very specific,
it's an idiolect,

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:21.530
it's one person's accent.

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:23.800
But, it's absolutely flawless.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:25.250
[Narrator] When doing a British accent,

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:28.080
there are a few basic
things to keep in mind.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:30.920
Posture wise the tongue
tips tend to be pointier

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:33.253
jaw tends to be a bit higher.

00:03:34.550 --> 00:03:37.333
I would listen particularly
for sounds like goat,

00:03:38.170 --> 00:03:43.083
the o-sound where it's
like hold, gold, hope.

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:46.220
I tend to use a bit more pitch,

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:47.720
tend to use pitch for emphasis

00:03:47.720 --> 00:03:49.310
a bit more so that'll get you started.

00:03:49.310 --> 00:03:51.400
[Narrator] There were many
types of British accents.

00:03:51.400 --> 00:03:53.790
And some tend to be more
difficult than others.

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:56.390
One thing that comes up for some reason

00:03:56.390 --> 00:03:58.500
is that a lot of Americans seem

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:01.060
to really struggle with is Welsh accents.

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:02.860
A lot of it is a lack of exposure.

00:04:02.860 --> 00:04:04.690
It's like Welsh, what
does that even sound like

00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:05.920
for a lot of Americans?

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:08.430
Unless you've gone to see a
production of "Under Milk Wood"

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:09.857
or something like that.

00:04:09.857 --> 00:04:13.820
You probably haven't been
exposed to lots of Welsh accents.

00:04:13.820 --> 00:04:15.960
There's also one type of British accent

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:17.630
that actors tend to lean on.

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:19.799
One of the first things that
you'll see American actors do

00:04:19.799 --> 00:04:23.507
is kind of straighten up,
yes, I've got to be like this.

00:04:23.507 --> 00:04:27.080
And there's an association
with the kind of fanciness.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:30.120
Sometimes you'll see British
actors kinda get soft

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:33.620
and floppy and lean back 'cause
American are all like that.

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:36.880
Which is adding a particular character

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:40.170
and attitude on top of an accent.

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:42.320
And I think it's really important

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:45.310
not to rely on those sorts of things.

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:48.710
And to realize that people are people.

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:52.430
And character is to a large
extent, independent of accent.

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:54.170
[Narrator] To perfect an American accent

00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:56.910
you need to focus on one key letter.

00:04:56.910 --> 00:05:01.370
A huge thing is getting American r-sounds.

00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:05.850
And there's two things that
can be challenging there.

00:05:05.850 --> 00:05:08.510
One of them is just making
sure they're all there.

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:11.360
And unstressed syllables
like at the end of words

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:14.683
like other and another,
mother, sister, brother,

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:17.263
you know, writer, baker.

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:19.820
We tend to skip right over those.

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:21.373
Carl! Carl!

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:27.750
American r's usually
are made for most people

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:30.350
with the sides of the
tongue kind of bunching up.

00:05:30.350 --> 00:05:33.130
It's a really odd thing
to do with your tongue.

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:35.940
And it can feel very awkward and clumsy

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:37.960
and it involves a lot of muscularity.

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:39.580
[Narrator] Regardless of your skills,

00:05:39.580 --> 00:05:43.090
sometimes your true accent
will pop up unexpectedly.

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:46.500
Getting very emotional, yelling,

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:48.013
also being tired or drunk,

00:05:49.200 --> 00:05:51.830
these are times when
accents do tend to slip.

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:54.860
I killed Bethany, my old
girl friend with a nail gun.

00:05:54.860 --> 00:05:57.470
[Narrator] So how long does
it take to master an accent?

00:05:57.470 --> 00:05:59.311
It just depends, it really does.

00:05:59.311 --> 00:06:00.990
The more time the better.

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:04.080
If you have six weeks
before shooting starts

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:06.940
or before rehearsals start for a play

00:06:06.940 --> 00:06:09.460
and you work on it really really hard,

00:06:09.460 --> 00:06:12.303
ideally with a really
qualified dialect coach,

00:06:13.410 --> 00:06:15.850
you stand a pretty good shot
of getting to a good place.

00:06:15.850 --> 00:06:18.344
Yes sir, I will give it my full attention.

00:06:18.344 --> 00:06:19.644
Our work is finished here.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:21.793
Hers has just begun.

